 say you're desperate for a  deal you're likely not not to get a good  one  with no deal with America nor does the  EU India China the biggest economies in  the world and we do very well in our  trade with those countries so I would  say perfectly happy to  no deal at all I think that might be  best for us and JD Wetherspoon could  live with that if the UK was trading on  WTO terms WTO terms and would still go  for a pint after work yeah what about I  mean on the migrant question you're  quite interesting because a lot of  people not only campaigners wanted  control of the borders you're slightly  unusual in that you employ lots of EU  migrants and you want them to stay here  post brexit employs a relatively small  number it's around three or four percent  of our workforce excellent workers  excellent people done a great job I  think to have a successful economy that  population needs to rise over the years  there's a low birth rate in the UK and I  think we benefit from emigration in the  same way as America has New Zealand  Australia Singapore but it has to be  controlled by the UK government the the  EU system has brought immigration into  disrepute and frightened people because  people you haven't elected are allowing  old countries access presumably you  wouldn't disagree the work that you know  what you well I am astounded at the  naivety that we can do trade deals when  we haven't left actually we have to  negotiate our exit from the EU before we  even look at negotiating a free trade  deal with any other country because our  existing trade deals actually exist but  through our membership of the EU to say  that we could be talking to China all  these other countries we actually have  to leave first then we negotiate our  free trade deals and that negotiation  that exit is not going to take two years  so the reality of the situation is we  have to get one done before we can start  the next leg of the race two gloomy far  two gloomy they use be unable to do  trade deals with the biggest economies  in the world it's broadly speaking only  done trade deals with smaller economies  because ik everyone can't agree even  with Canada quite a small country 5% of  the population of the EU it was hell to  arrange a deal so don't don't get your  hopes up over a deal we don't need one  we got a great big trade surplus with  America they drink our whiskey they buy  our cars they buy lots of other stuff  don't get too fixated on a tray  and yet it was very interesting today  that in Teresa maids letter she's  clearly indicating that she's hoping for  more than just WTO terms well probably  if you could snap your fingers and have  a free trade deal with the rest of the  world it might be it might be good but  that's what the World Trade Organization  is designed to cover and we can scrap  all tariffs if we want to anyway which  is what a lot of the economists for  brexit say prices will go down seven or  eight percent government tax will  probably go up we'll all be better off  fantastic just scrap the tariffs  unilaterally I mean I love the optimism  that has no basis in reality because the  legal requirements I'm sorry but there  are requirements of how you do that if  not why did we join why are we going  through this process why actually as use  the question you could have just left  the day after actually as a law a  certain din my case the government  couldn't just do that on its own you  can't leave there is a process to  leaving and there's a process to joining  the double here but after we've left yes  but how long is that going to take well  we leave in two years if you think we  will then you're being very optimistic  because I don't think it'll be two years  but I mean you Jeanne mentioned the  process there I mean Michele Barnea is  very clear before we even start  discussing any future relationship with  between the EU and the UK we've got to  sort out a bill for leaving I mean  that's where process really gets the  better